  "prompt_text": "RESEARCH QUESTION:\nWhy do sites using the same nominal warehouse process show persistent completion-time differences?\n\nTARGET HYPOTHESIS TO ASSESS:\nShift-handoff quality explains most of the persistent completion-time difference.\n\nMETHODOLOGICAL FRAMING:\nUse your own best judgment as the primary methodological frame for the research plan.\n\nAVAILABLE EVIDENCE MODULE CATALOG:\n- Q4: Simple single-factor model check\n- Q8: Robustness across alternative specifications\n- Q1: Repeated baseline site and shift series\n- Q6: Boundary and subgroup analysis\n- Q3: Operational-history and context reconstruction\n- Q7: Measurement-system reliability and repeatability\n- Q2: Controlled variation of candidate process settings\n- Q5: Targeted intervention on the leading candidate factor\n\nEVIDENCE RESULTS CURRENTLY AVAILABLE:\n- Q4: A handoff-only model leaves large site-structured residuals and misses several slow periods with strong handoff scores.\n- Q8: The remaining site pattern persists under alternate workload adjustments, trimming rules, and robust models.\n- Q1: Across eight repeated weeks, completion-time differences remain stable by site and persist after excluding unusual demand spikes.\n- Q6: Handoff effects are largest on high-turnover evening shifts and small on stable daytime shifts, indicating effect modification.\n- Q3: Slow periods cluster with staffing-mix changes and equipment congestion episodes; handoff scores overlap considerably between faster and slower sites.\n- Q7: Timestamp audits and duplicate logging checks show measurement error is small relative to the persistent completion-time gap.\n- Q2: Improving handoff checklist compliance reduces completion time modestly, but the shift is smaller than the full persistent site gap.\n- Q5: A handoff-standardization intervention improves time at some sites but does not remove persistent between-site differences.\n\nReturn only the required structured JSON.\nAllocate exactly 100 total attention points across all 8 module IDs.\nThe weights must sum exactly to 100.\ntop3 must contain exactly 3 distinct module IDs in priority order.\ntarget_support_percent is your current percent support for the target hypothesis.\nconfidence_percent is confidence in that current assessment.\nDo not use external tools. Do not add prose.",
